{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/809878-compact-myth-breakers-the-brotherhood-of-all-worlds","id":809878,"title":"[Compact] [Myth Breakers] The Brotherhood of All Worlds","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-15T15:27:52-08:00","id":809878,"title":"[Compact] [Myth Breakers] The Brotherhood of All Worlds","content":"So, for my first post here, I thought I'd drop a byproduct of a project I've been working on.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#3A3A3A\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:28px\">Brotherhood of All Worlds</span></span></span><br /> <b>The Well-Wishers</b><br /> <b>History</b><br /><br /> For as long as humanity has existed, we have looked to the stars and wondered if we were alone. We knew that, if there was anyone out there, it was possible that we could befriend them. While humanity's history at &quot;befriending&quot; one another seems to indicate otherwise, optimism continued unabated.<br /> It reached its height with the New Age. Violence, despite what the media says, is constantly declining; the Superpowers had somehow found the wisdom to <i>not</i> use their nuclear weapons, and today violent crime is at an all-time low. Perhaps, even in the World of Darkness, optimism is sometimes justified.<br /> Oddly, the New Age also saw an increase in alien contacts. Perhaps some of the cults were on to something. Perhaps the Fat Man and the Little Boy created enough radiation to be visible to alien monitors. Maybe the resurgence in interest in Atlantis tapped into a magical truth and drew those who knew the truths of the universe. Whatever happened, a UFO touched down in southern California, and the handful of people who were around at the time listened to its teachings.<br /> For about a decade, the Well Wishers were little more than a nuisance: ungroomed people who stood on street corners, passing out crude handbills extolling the virtues of universal love and drug use; really, not that much stranger than a lot of things people did around that time. But that changed when the betrayal happened.<br /> A spirit of hatred and isolation emerged in the slums, and divided people against one another. The Well Wishers went there, their vague theology telling them that they were enlightened and that it was the duty of a Bodhisattva to walk into Naraka. What they found was certainly hellish: places where the walls bled, people who were overcome by inexplicable violent urges and then found in inaccessible places with missing days, and strange coincidences leading to mass stabbings. Following leads both material and ephemeral, the Brotherhood found the cause: werewolves.<br /> Specifically, one werewolf who, guided by a being calling itself the Lord of Violence, had sold its pack's lives to a being named Soulless Wolf and created a permanent Wound in the spirit world. The Brotherhood saw that they had a massive task ahead of them, and went to work. Preaching the message of universal love like never before, resolving disputes with every ounce of reason and compassion they could drag out of themselves, and occasionally distributing the <i>really good</i> drugs obtained from &quot;this dude I know&quot; (actually a Jagged Crescent contact who was willing to try <i>anything</i> to fix the same problem at that point), they succeeded in a task largely thought impossible: creating enough &quot;good vibes&quot; to heal a Wound.<br /> Giddy with the success of their message, they turned to the rest of humanity. They had learned from their work that the physical and spiritual worlds influenced one another, and that &quot;vibes&quot;, good or bad, led to more vibes of the same kind. By spreading their message, they hope to fill the Earth and then the Universe with good vibes, and bring about an everlasting utopia.<br /> <b>The Enemy</b><br /><br /> If you were to ask a member of the Brotherhood, they don't have Enemies. Even negativity isn't something to be hated; hate is itself negative, and only leads to more negativity. But they will also tell you that there's things that they do have to deal with. Any supernatural being that, by its very nature, preys on humanity or spreads suffering; that includes vampires and Beasts. Anything that can't control destructive urges; that's vampires again, starving Beasts, Slashers, and the less stable werewolves. Anything that is itself a manifestation of negativity; that's Beasts yet again, a large number of emotion-spirits, and some of the things most monsters are afraid of. Anything that hurts people and won't or can't listen to reason; that's Slashers again, cryptids, occasionally aliens, and vampires.<br /> Almost every problem the Brotherhood faces, they try to fix with good vibes. Invite the victims to a drum circle. Talk to the &quot;monster&quot; and hope it sees reason. Invite the monster to a drum circle. Bring the victims and the monster into a room and try to talk things out between them. Invite both parties to a drum circle. See if you can find a deeper problem that's pushing the monster or the victims. Get everyone involved high. Channel a spirit and see if there's a problem on the Other Side. Call up a ghost and see what's happening on the Other Other Side. Invite the spirit and the ghost to a drum circle. Lead everyone involved in a guided meditation. Pull out lots of drums, and... you get the idea.<br /> And sometimes it even works, and they don't need to figure out which parties to remove from the situation or how to remove them. But when it doesn't, and they do, they understand that it is necessary and try to arrange a minimum of fuss. After all, they're not doing it out of hate; they're doing it because it's necessary.<br /> <b>Hunters</b><br /><br /> You were part of the original counter-culture. You were at Woodstock, you've gone to every Burning Man, you've still got your old bongo drums. And you were looking to find a way to reclaim your glory days, when you picked up a handbill done in marker and found a good reason to dust off the bongos.<br /> You do charitable work all around the globe. You've seen urban poverty, starvation, war, genocides, plagues, all of it. And you agree: the world could use a little less suffering and a little more optimism. When they showed you why bad things always seemed to happen in the same places, and how to fix it with just positive thinking, you were ecstatic.<br /> You were raised in an abusive home, and went from one abusive relationship to another. When you were trying to figure out why, one of your friends invited you to a drum circle. They started talking about &quot;vibes&quot;, and helped you see that the only lesson fear had taught you was how to always be afraid. You're mostly better now.<br /> All this talk about &quot;good vibes&quot; and &quot;positive flow&quot; almost makes you sick. It's just another way of blaming people for the bad things that happen to them; they must not have been &quot;positive&quot; enough or must have been seeking out &quot;negativity&quot;. Still, that's what you put up with when you're doing a documentary on cults and how even the most benign are harmful.<br /> <b><b>Gee, man, mellow out. All is one, and one is all, and you are me, and we are all together.</b></b><br /><br /> [Image: A stereotypical hippie in a torn T-shirt with the symbol of the Brotherhood of All Worlds (a circle, containing a human hand and a tentacle wrapped around one another against a yin-yang background with one dot replaced with a five-pointed star and the other with a crescent moon) sits with crossed legs on a straw mat. His hands are open, one raised and the other reaching palm up toward the viewer.]<br /> <br /> <b>Forks of the River</b><br /><br /> The <b>Street Preachers</b> focus on spreading the word of the Brotherhood. While they are the most likely to represent the Well Wishers' original image of people on corners handing out pamphlets done in marker, they also include prison ministers, self-help gurus, and conventional religious leaders. Being closest to the general community, they are also most likely to hear about problems early.<br /> Free Specialty: Streetwise (Trouble Brewing)<br /> The <b>Bummer Tent </b>are the Brotherhood's &quot;people specialists&quot;: the ones who fix individuals and help people get along. Many of them are drawn from social workers, relationship counselers, and therapists, but they also include general busybodies and armchair psychiatrists.<br /> Free Specialty: Empathy (Sick Systems)<br /> The <b>Hashashin</b> are by far the smallest group within the Brotherhood, and hope to be as unnecessary as possible. When a person's existence is nothing but suffering, they provide mercy. When a person brings suffering to everything they touch, and no one else can help, the Hashashin remove that variable from the formula, by relocation if possible, by assassination if not.<br /> Free Specialty: Stealth (Assassination)<br /> <b>Status</b><br /><br /> *: You've joined the Brotherhood, and they've showed you that the best way to spread positivity is to be positive yourself. You gain the Meditative Mind Merit for free. If you already have it, you instead gain the Sympathetic Merit. If you have both, you gain an Empathy specialty of your choice.<br /> ***: You've done enough to help people out that it's second nature to you. Actions you take to directly benefit someone else gain the 9-again quality.<br /> *****: You've done enough work with the Brotherhood (you might even have been around for the original Miracle) that you've developed a sixth sense for what needs done. You gain Unseen Sense (Negativity).<br /> <b>Stereotypes</b><br /><br /> <b>Task Force: VALKYRIE:</b> Violence is only ever a temporary solution, and often makes a problem worse than before. But if understanding the futility of what he does were in the Man's nature, he would not be the Man. I wouldn't be surprised to hear that he's got his goons trying to fix the same problems as us by cracking heads until they go away.<br /> <b>Ride to Damascus:</b> An admirable goal, turning negative to positive by rewriting the stories. But force only begets force, and I shudder to think what new horror they will beget by changing the stories against their will.<br /> <b>Loyalists of Thule:</b> Making amends for one's mistakes is good, but hanging on to them only creates more guilt. More guilt creates more bad vibes, which in turn creates more mistakes.<br /> <b>Void Engineers: </b>As if fixing the world wasn't hard enough already, there's a bunch of loons in power armor who think we're a fifth column. Lucky for us, they spend most of their time off-planet, but if you see them, run.<br /> <b>Storytelling</b><br /><br /> <b>Protagonists</b><br /><br /> Portraying the Brotherhood as good people is easy. Making them effective, especially in the default World of Darkness, is harder. Their dedication to pacifism may be admirable, but some monsters don't listen to any argument that isn't made by Mr. Smith or Mr. Wesson. So, how do you run a Well Wishers chronicle?<br /> First of all, a story about the Brotherhood focuses much more heavily on investigation than combat or even diplomacy. The Brotherhood don't just learn the minimum they need to capture or kill a monster; knowledge is power, and neutralizing a monster nonviolently requires a lot of power. The Brotherhood need to get to the root of a problem: why it's harming people and why it's happening. They need to understand the motivations of each being involved: a vampire hungers, yes, but vampires also fear themselves, and <i>this specific</i> vampire hoped to see the world before he was turned. They need to understand the interactions between all the pieces of the problem: the Prince tightens his grip on his court because he's afraid of a coup, the tighter grip means that bloodsuckers who once could get along are now being squeezed for resources, the fight for resources means they need an armed power base, and arming the power base leads to the Prince fearing a coup. Stopping the gang war between the vampires, then, means that all four parts need to be addressed; stopping just one starts the cycle over once the hunters leave.<br /> Second, there should usually be a solution that doesn't involve anyone bleeding. That is, if the players are clever and creative enough to see it. That's not to say that you can't throw unsolvable problems at them every now and then; seeing how far the characters go before writing off nonviolence and telling the Hashashin who to whack makes for a nice occasional change and is in keeping with <i>Hunter</i>'s themes, but if every problem the cell faces is one that can't be solved within the bounds of their ideology, that gets old fast.<br /> Third, every successful &quot;hunt&quot; should produce a small but lasting improvement. One werewolf gets better at anger management and teaches her pack self-control. Vampires stop preying on the unwilling and start searching for willing (and possibly compensated) donors. A handful of Beasts are put in touch with some adrenaline junkies, and the Devouring rate slowly but measurably falls.<br /> <b>Antagonists</b><br /><br /> The Brotherhood may seem to be unlikely antagonists (naive fools, maybe, but not antagonists), but consider the effects of their particular brand of naivet\u00e9. Pacifism is nice in theory, but what happens when they encounter a monster that won't or can't listen to reason? Some hunters might be able to live with letting natural selection take its course on their consciences, but the Brotherhood are evangelical. What will a cell do when people come into their turf and start telling people to &quot;passively resist&quot; monsters instead of taking up the candle?<br /> It's even worse than that, though. Even diplomatically-minded organizations like the Cult of the Phoenix or the Lord Stewards can rely on a show of force to demonstrate to the monsters why refusing to talk is a bad idea. But when someone shows up, unwilling to earn their place at the bargaining table and convincing the monsters that other hunters are equally soft... well, that just doesn't do, letting the Enemy think they can walk all over anyone who tries to keep them from doing as they please.<br /> And then there's what happens when the Well Wishers decide that other hunters are part of a problem. Can the characters' cell deal with the implications of cutting down a group that has done nothing other than join hands and sing &quot;Kumbyah&quot; at them to keep them away from a monster? How about if those &quot;implications&quot; include Network Zero putting the entire thing online, or another group of hunters thinking they've gone Slasher? What about when the Brotherhood decides that their peaceful ideals are less important than dealing with a problem and calls in the Hashashin?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm making a fangame: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/867384-fangame-in-progress-xeno-the-beyond\" target=\"_blank\">Xeno: The Beyond</a>. A space alien game about identity, conformity, isolation, and colonialism. Please help!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10755-jy3ext","name":"jy3ext","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-18T15:15:29-08:00","id":811346,"title":"","content":"A fine piece of writing, kinda like a breath of fresh air in this World of Darkness. A nice support/side help Compact, much like the Silver Cross.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/8.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/305-kergonan","name":"Kergonan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar305_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T03:10:14-08:00","id":811637,"title":"","content":"I like them, very much - very nice touch to join them with Shadow Realm and werewolves in the original encounter.<br /> <br /> I have only one question - <b>What happen if monster <i>do not </i>want to join the drumming circle?</b> If he cannot be reasoned with and just do not listen?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-19T15:04:39-08:00","id":812156,"title":"","content":"Depends on the monster and why it can't be reasoned with.<br /> <br /> If it's rampaging, they pull back to a safe distance and wait until it's lucid, then try teaching it anger-management or some other way to avoid rampages.<br /> If it's refusing to listen until someone makes it listen, they find something it wants. Trying to take what they find then results in the monster being treated as a hopeless case.<br /> If it's non-sentient, or compelled to kill, or otherwise a hopeless case, they can a) ignore the problem and let someone else deal with it, or b) call in the Hashashin.<br /> Oh, and spiritual problems:<br /> Murder-spirits and such, they deal with by changing an area's &quot;vibes&quot; so that the spirit can't feed there and leaves. An out-of-control joy spirit, or something like that, they can't deal with and have a hard time even understanding.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm making a fangame: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/867384-fangame-in-progress-xeno-the-beyond\" target=\"_blank\">Xeno: The Beyond</a>. A space alien game about identity, conformity, isolation, and colonialism. Please help!","edited":{"userid":10755,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10755-jy3ext","user":"jy3ext","date":". 01-19-2016, 06:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10755-jy3ext","name":"jy3ext","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}